kinstall-core's fixtures, driven by testing/selftest.sh through harness.c.

root/ stands in for an install's target: the image's own kde_settings.conf,
/etc/xdg/kdeglobals and /etc/kdos/xkb.conf (copied from fs/ — refresh them when
those change), a passwd naming the sddm account, one accent's login-screen set
under usr/share/kdos/greeter/amber, and empty kbd keymaps for the console
keymap search. The selftest copies it before each run, so a writer never edits
the fixture itself.

pagedraw.c draws one kinstall page with no terminal, linking pages.c with
kinstall-core: it prints the page's body rectangle, the whole frame as text
and `# cuts N`, the text draws that lost the end of their string. Its
PAGEDRAW_* variables stand in a disk, a mounted partition, a processor's
name and a running or failed install, as its header lists. zoneinfo/ is a
three-zone zone1970.tab, none of them UTC, for the 80x33 sections that check
the Time page's choosing; zoneinfo-long/ holds zone names wider than the
Time page's zone list, for the section that draws every page at 80x33 with
the longest values and refuses any `# cuts` but 0.

copy-progress.txt is a real rsync --info=progress2 --no-inc-recursive stream,
each line prefixed with the seconds since rsync started, thinned to a line
each 50 ms and keeping every line without file counts. The selftest feeds it
to `harness copy` at its own times and re-timed, and bounds the time left the
Copy step's note shows.

live-shape.txt is the live medium's tree in rsync's copy order (the ISO's
system.sfs, listed and sorted by path), as runs of 128 entries: entries and
bytes per run. A run with a third field, L, is extra names of hard-linked
files (15% of the bytes, as on the live root), which rsync -H counts in its
total and makes as links at no copy cost; the stream advances the file
count over them and no bytes or time. The selftest builds progress2 streams
from it at several costs per byte and per file, and bounds the time left on
the tree the installer actually copies, given the link names' bytes and,
to show the bound needs them, without. Refresh it when the tree's shape
changes a lot.

live-tree.txt is the same tree by kind of entry, in rsync's own order (files
before directories in each directory, each directory followed by what is in
it), as runs of 128 entries: regular files, later names of hard-linked files,
symbolic links, directories, and the bytes of the files and of the later
names. It was made from `unsquashfs -lln` of the ISO's system.sfs, with the
later names found by inode in the built tree. `harness copy` builds the
copy's profile from it as probe_payload() does from the live root, and the
selftest copies it at costs per kind fitted to the progress the rig's GUI
install showed. Refresh it with live-shape.txt.

`harness copy` also reads a KINSTALL_COPY_TRACE file (kinstall writes one when
the variable names a file): its `# prof` line stands in for a tree, its lines
are replayed at their recorded times, and each `# <copy> total= links=` line
starts the next copy of those figures, printing `# <copy>` before its notes.
With KI_HARNESS_TICKS in its environment it also gives the ticks kinstall's
reader gives each KI_COPY_TICK_S with no line, each note of a tick printed as
`@<seconds> <note>` between the notes of the lines around it.

Eight such files come from the live medium's graphical installer on the rig.
two30.trace, two30b.trace, fresh30.trace, fresh30c.trace and e2e10-30.trace
are installs onto an empty 30 GiB disk, each a root copy and then slot B's;
one12.trace, fresh12.trace and e2e10-12.trace are installs onto 12 GiB, one
slot. two30b's slot B copy ends in small files that its estimate reads a
little high; two30's and fresh30c's slot B copies have an estimate at 0.65
to 0.8 of the real time through their first half, which sets the low floor
the selftest gives the plain replays; e2e10-30's slot B copy has a stretch
from 13 s to 17 s of its 28 that copies slower than the rest, through which
its estimate stands at 1.3 to 1.4 times the real time, which sets the high
bound the selftest gives that copy. fresh12, fresh30 and fresh30c come from
one live medium's GUI installs framed once a second, and e2e10-12 and
e2e10-30 from another's, so their replays can be set against the frames.
All eight are thinned as copy-progress.txt is: every `#` line and every
line without file counts kept, and of the lines with counts one each 50 ms,
with the last before each line without counts and the last of each copy.
The selftest replays each copy with ticks, one12's root copy re-timed to
two GUI installs on 12 GiB (the notes those runs showed, MiB over rate), and
each real copy slowed two, three and five times with a running line in each
second left empty, and bounds the time left a front end shows on them,
framed at twenty phases of the second. The time left's constants in
kinstall-core.h each sit inside the range on which every replay but
e2e10-30's slot B passes.
